<li><a id="n4"></a>The Friedman rule (Milton Friedman, &ldquo;The Optimum  Quantity of Money,&rdquo; in <em>The Optimum Quantity of Money and Other Essays</em>,  Chicago: Aldine, 1969) calls for equating the opportunity cost of holding money  to the marginal social cost of creating money. When money does not bear  interest, the Friedman rule also has implications for the optimal inflation  rate, which are beyond the scope of these remarks. See Logan (2023).</li>
